    question: "You returned a pointer from a function. Who owns that memory?",
    slug: "you-returned-a-pointer-from-a-function-who-owns-that-memory",
    shortDescription: "A pointer is an object that stores the address of another object or function (for function pointers). It supports indirect access and is central to arrays, dynamic memory, linked lists, callbacks, and systems programming...",
    explanation: "Give the direct answer first, then explain the mechanism and one practical example. For coding questions, state time/space complexity and discuss edge cases. For debugging questions, explain how you would reproduce, isolate, diagnose, fix, and verify the problem.\n\nWhat the interviewer is testing: C language fundamentals, pointers and memory safety, undefined behavior awareness, problem-solving ability, debugging skill, and practical systems-level reasoning.",
    sampleAnswer: "A pointer is an object that stores the address of another object or function (for function pointers). It supports indirect access and is central to arrays, dynamic memory, linked lists, callbacks, and systems programming. A pointer can be null and can become dangling when the pointed object’s lifetime ends. Pointer arithmetic is defined within an appropriate array object and scales by the pointed-to type size.",
    detailedAnswer: "Evidence label: [FOLLOW-UP]\n\nAnswer: A pointer is an object that stores the address of another object or function (for function pointers). It supports indirect access and is central to arrays, dynamic memory, linked lists, callbacks, and systems programming. A pointer can be null and can become dangling when the pointed object’s lifetime ends. Pointer arithmetic is defined within an appropriate array object and scales by the pointed-to type size.\n\nExample: int value = 10; int p = &value; printf(“%d”, p); // prints 10\n\nInterview Tip: Give the direct answer first, then explain the mechanism and one practical example. For coding questions, state time/space complexity and discuss edge cases. For debugging questions, explain how you would reproduce, isolate, diagnose, fix, and verify the problem.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing: C language fundamentals, pointers and memory safety, undefined behavior awareness, problem-solving ability, debugging skill, and practical systems-level reasoning.\n\nKey Points to Remember: Be precise about object lifetime, allocation ownership, array/pointer differences, integer/type rules, synchronization, and what the C standard actually guarantees. Do not present compiler-specific behavior as portable C behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions: What happens internally? What is the edge case? What is the time/space complexity? What happens on allocation failure? Is the behavior defined by the C standard? How would you debug it in production?\n\nCommon Mistakes: Confusing arrays with pointers, forgetting the terminating ‘\\0’, dereferencing invalid pointers, ignoring allocation failure, using unsafe string operations, assuming sizeof is a runtime string length, or claiming volatile provides atomicity/thread safety. ====================================================================================================",
